<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plotinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 204–270</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plotinus was a Greek-speaking philosopher, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, who studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammonius-saccas">Ammonius Saccas</a> and from 244 taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His treatises, edited by his pupil Porphyry as the Enneads, describe all reality as flowing from a single source, the One. He is regarded as the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a>, which shaped later pagan, Christian and Islamic thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plotinus.jpg" alt="Plotinus" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plotinos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Plotinus's pupil and biographer Porphyry records in his Life of Plotinus that he died at sixty-six at the end of the second year of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a>, and so calculates that he was born in the thirteenth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a>, about 204.</p><p>Eunapius reported that he was born in Lyco, which could mean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> or a Lycopolis in the Delta. This has led to speculation that his family was Hellenized Egyptian, Greek or Roman. The scholar A. H. Armstrong writes that all that can be said with reasonable certainty is that Greek was his normal language and that he had a Greek education.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Plotinus took up philosophy at twenty-eight, around 232, and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. He was dissatisfied with every teacher until an acquaintance suggested he hear the self-taught Platonist Ammonius Saccas. After the lecture he declared, this is the man I was looking for, and he remained with Ammonius for eleven years. Around the age of thirty-eight he decided to investigate the teachings of Persian and Indian philosophers and joined the army of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordian-iii">Gordian III</a> on its march against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 242–243. The campaign failed, and Plotinus reached safety in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> only with great difficulty.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> he gained the respect of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> and his wife Salonina. He once tried to persuade Gallienus to rebuild an abandoned settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> as a City of Philosophers, whose inhabitants would live under the constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s Laws; the subsidy was never granted, for reasons Porphyry did not know.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>From about 253 until a few months before his death Plotinus wrote the essays that became the Enneads, a title meaning a group of nine. They were notes and essays he used in lectures and debates. His eyesight was poor, his handwriting atrocious and his spelling careless, so Porphyry polished and edited them into their modern form. Plotinus described three fundamental principles: the One, the Intellect and the Soul. The One is totally transcendent, without division or multiplicity, beyond being and non-being, and he identified it with the Good and with Beauty. All things flow from it by emanation without diminishing it.</p><p>He held that true happiness does not depend on worldly fortune. He died in seclusion on an estate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> left to him by his friend Zethos, and Eustochius reported his last words: Try to raise the divine in yourselves to the divine in the all. His thought influenced pagan, Jewish, Christian and Islamic thinkers, among them Augustine, the Fatimid teacher Hamid al-Din al-Kirmani and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Plotinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Claudius Ptolemy wrote the Almagest and the Geography there in the second century, <b>Plotinus</b> studied under the philosopher Ammonius Saccas before founding Neoplatonism in Rome, and Hero of Alexandria described machines and devices.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Neoplatonists such as <b>Plotinus</b> also opposed the Gnostics, rejecting their radical dualism.</blockquote><a href="/religion/gnosticism">Gnosticism · Community &amp; Society</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minturnae">Minturno</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.27,13.75&amp;year=246" aria-label="Minturno on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Plotinus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5875 3990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5875 3990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.27,13.75&amp;year=246">What was here in 246 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Withdraw within yourself, and examine yourself. If you do not yet therein discover beauty, do as the artist, who cuts off, polishes, purifies until he has adorned his statue with all the marks of beauty.&#8221;</p><footer>Enneads, 1.6 (Of Beauty), 9 &#183; tr. Kenneth Sylvan Guthrie, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42930/pg42930-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Remove from your soul, therefore, all that is superfluous, straighten out all that is crooked, purify and illuminate what is obscure, and do not cease perfecting your statue until the divine resplendence of virtue shines forth upon your sight…&#8221;</p><footer>Enneads, 1.6 (Of Beauty), 9 &#183; tr. Kenneth Sylvan Guthrie, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42930/pg42930-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
